ARCHITECTURE_FINAL.md
SeatLock — Architecture & System Design
1. Architecture Style

SeatLock follows a modular monolith architecture.

Client / Frontend
       |
       v
+-----------------------+
|      Express API      |
| Auth / Validation     |
| Rate Limit / Logging  |
+----------+------------+
           |
     +-----+-----+
     |           |
     v           v
 Booking      Payment
 Module       Module
     |           |
     v           v
 PostgreSQL    Stripe
     |
     +------------------+
     |                  |
     v                  v
  Outbox              Redis
     |                  |
     v                  +---- Cache
   BullMQ               +---- Redlock
     |
     v
  Worker
2. Application Layers
Routes
  ↓
Middleware
  ↓
Controllers
  ↓
Services
  ↓
Repositories
  ↓
PostgreSQL / Redis / External APIs
Routes: HTTP method and URL mapping.
Middleware: authentication, validation, rate limiting, correlation IDs, logging and error handling.
Controllers: translate HTTP requests into service calls and responses.
Services: contain business logic.
Repositories: contain database access and concurrency-sensitive queries.
3. Main Domain Model
User
 |
 +---- Booking
         |
         +---- BookingSeat
         |       |
         |       +---- ShowSeat
         |                |
         |                +---- Seat
         |
         +---- Payment

Movie
 |
 +---- Show
        |
        +---- Screen
               |
               +---- Seat

Seat is the physical seat definition. ShowSeat represents the state of a seat for a particular show.

Example:

Seat A1

Show 1 → BOOKED
Show 2 → AVAILABLE
Show 3 → HELD
4. Seat State Machine
             +-------------+
             |  AVAILABLE  |
             +------+------+
                    |
                  booking
                    |
                    v
             +-------------+
             |    HELD     |
             +------+------+
                    |
             +------+------+
             |             |
          payment        expiry
             |             |
             v             v
       +-----------+  +-------------+
       |  BOOKED   |  |  AVAILABLE  |
       +-----------+  +-------------+

A HELD seat has an expiry time. If payment is not completed before expiry, the background worker releases the seat.

5. Pessimistic Locking

The primary booking implementation uses PostgreSQL row-level locking:

SELECT
    id,
    "showId",
    "seatId",
    status,
    "holdToken",
    "holdExpiresAt",
    version,
    price
FROM "ShowSeat"
WHERE id = $1
FOR UPDATE;

Flow:

BEGIN TRANSACTION
       |
       v
Lock ShowSeat
       |
       v
Check status
   |         |
AVAILABLE   HELD/BOOKED
   |         |
   v         v
Create      Reject
Booking
   |
   v
Seat → HELD
   |
   v
Create BookingSeat
   |
   v
Create OutboxEvent
   |
   v
COMMIT

If multiple requests target the same row, PostgreSQL serializes access.

Expected race:

10 concurrent requests

201 → 1
409 → 9
6. Deterministic Lock Ordering

For multiple seats, selected ShowSeat IDs are sorted before locking.

Without deterministic ordering:

Transaction A: A1 → A2
Transaction B: A2 → A1

This can create a deadlock cycle. With deterministic ordering both transactions attempt A1 → A2.

7. Optimistic Locking

ShowSeat.version is used for optimistic concurrency control.

Initial:
version = 1
status  = AVAILABLE

Two requests read version 1. Only one update should win:

Request A → update WHERE version = 1
Request B → update WHERE version = 1

Winner:
version 1 → 2
AVAILABLE → HELD

The losing request detects the version conflict and fails/retries according to the service logic.

8. Redis / Redlock
             Load Balancer
                  |
        +---------+---------+
        |                   |
        v                   v
    API Instance A      API Instance B
        |                   |
        +---------+---------+
                  |
                  v
                Redis

Redlock provides distributed coordination across application instances. Redis locking does not replace PostgreSQL correctness; PostgreSQL remains the authoritative source of persistent seat state.

9. Payment Architecture
Booking
   |
   | PENDING + HELD seats
   v
Create PaymentIntent
   |
   v
Stripe
   |
Customer Payment
   |
   v
Stripe Webhook
   |
   v
Signature Verification
   |
   v
Webhook Idempotency
   |
   v
Payment = SUCCEEDED
   |
   v
Saga Confirmation
   |
   v
Booking = CONFIRMED
Seats = BOOKED

The backend does not trust a client-side payment-success flag.

10. Stripe Webhook Security

The Stripe webhook route is registered before the global JSON parser because signature verification requires the original raw request body.

Raw HTTP body
      |
      v
constructEvent()
      |
   +--+--+
   |     |
 invalid valid
   |     |
 reject process
11. Webhook Idempotency

Stripe may retry webhook delivery.

Stripe Event
     |
     v
WebhookEvent
     |
     +---- Already processed?
             |
        +----+----+
        |         |
       YES        NO
        |         |
       Stop      Process
                  |
                  v
              Mark processed

The provider event ID is unique, preventing duplicate processing.

12. Payment Idempotency
Same key + same request
          |
          v
Return existing result
Same key + different request
          |
          v
Reject

This protects against retry-driven duplicate payment creation.

13. Saga Pattern

Payment and booking involve multiple systems, so one global ACID transaction cannot cover all of them.

Create Booking
      |
      v
Hold Seats
      |
      v
Create Payment
      |
      v
Payment Succeeded
      |
      v
Confirm Booking
      |
      v
Book Seats

If a later step fails:

Failure
  |
  v
Compensating Action
  |
  v
Release Seats
  |
  v
Cancel / Update Booking
14. Outbox Pattern

Important asynchronous events are written to PostgreSQL in the same transaction as the business state change.

BEGIN
 |
 +-- Update Booking / Seat
 |
 +-- Insert OutboxEvent
 |
COMMIT
 |
 v
Outbox Dispatcher
 |
 v
BullMQ
 |
 v
Worker

If the application crashes after commit, the event still exists in PostgreSQL and can be dispatched later.

15. BullMQ Worker
PostgreSQL
    |
    v
Outbox Event
    |
    v
Dispatcher
    |
    v
BullMQ / Redis
    |
    v
Worker
    |
    +---- Seat Release
    |
    +---- Other Background Jobs
16. Automatic Seat Release
Seat = HELD
Booking = PENDING
holdExpiresAt = future time
        |
        v
Delayed BullMQ job
        |
        v
Lock ShowSeat
        |
        v
Still HELD?
        |
   +----+----+
   |         |
  NO        YES
   |         |
Nothing   Hold expired?
             |
         +---+---+
         |       |
        NO      YES
         |       |
      Nothing    v
            Seat → AVAILABLE
            Booking → CANCELLED

The worker checks current database state before releasing the seat.

17. Cache-Aside
Request
   |
   v
 Redis
 /   \
HIT  MISS
 |     |
 v     v
Return PostgreSQL
       |
       v
   Store in Redis
       |
       v
     Return

A Redis lock protects cache population from a cache stampede. Seat availability is not treated as a normal cached source of truth.

18. Rate Limiting
Request
   |
   v
Redis Token Bucket
   |
 +--+--+
 |     |
Token  No Token
 |       |
 v       v
Allow   429

Different limits are configured for general, authentication, booking and payment operations.

19. Authentication
Register → bcrypt hash → User

Login → Verify password → Access Token + Refresh Token

Protected API → Verify JWT → req.user → Controller
20. Request Pipeline
Client
  |
  v
Correlation ID
  |
  v
Request Logger
  |
  v
Route
  |
  v
Authentication
  |
  v
Rate Limiter
  |
  v
Zod Validation
  |
  v
Controller
  |
  v
Service
  |
  v
Repository
  |
  v
Database / Redis / External API
  |
  v
Response

Errors are routed through centralized error middleware.

21. Observability
Request
   |
   v
Correlation ID
   |
   v
Pino Request Log
   |
   v
Business Logs
   |
   v
Error Logger
22. Graceful Shutdown
SIGTERM / SIGINT
       |
       v
Stop accepting new requests
       |
       v
Finish active work
       |
       v
Stop workers
       |
       v
Disconnect Prisma
       |
       v
Close Redis
       |
       v
Exit
23. Concurrency Test Architecture
                 Same ShowSeat
                      |
        +-------------+-------------+
        |             |             |
        v             v             v
     Request 1     Request 2    Request 10
        |             |             |
        +-------------+-------------+
                      |
                      v
              PostgreSQL Lock
                      |
               +------+------+
               |             |
               v             v
             Winner        Losers
              201            409

Current automated tests cover pessimistic concurrency, optimistic concurrency and payment idempotency.

24. Consistency Boundaries
Strong consistency

PostgreSQL transactions are used for:

Seat state.
Booking state.
Booking-seat relationships.
Payment database state.
Eventual consistency

Acceptable for:

Outbox dispatch.
Background jobs.
Cache.
Logs.
25. Production Deployment Architecture
                    INTERNET
                       |
                       v
                  +---------+
                  |  Nginx  |
                  | TLS/SSL |
                  +----+----+
                       |
                       v
                +--------------+
                | Express API  |
                +------+-------+
                       |
          +------------+------------+
          |            |            |
          v            v            v
     PostgreSQL      Redis        Stripe
          |            |
          |            v
          |          BullMQ
          |            |
          |            v
          |          Worker
          |
          v
        Outbox

Final deployment components:

API Container
Worker Container
PostgreSQL
Redis
Nginx
26. Core System Invariants
Seat invariant
A ShowSeat can have at most one successful BookingSeat.
Hold invariant
An expired HELD seat must eventually become AVAILABLE.
Payment invariant
A Stripe payment event must not be applied twice.
Idempotency invariant
One idempotency key + one request fingerprint
must produce one logical operation.
Outbox invariant
A committed critical state change must have its
corresponding outbox event persisted in the same transaction.
27. End-to-End Flow
User
 |
 v
Login
 |
 v
JWT
 |
 v
Select Show + Seats
 |
 v
POST /bookings
 |
 v
Validate + Rate Limit
 |
 v
PostgreSQL Transaction
 |
 +-- Lock ShowSeats
 +-- Verify AVAILABLE
 +-- Create PENDING Booking
 +-- Set seats HELD
 +-- Create BookingSeats
 +-- Create OutboxEvent
 |
 v
COMMIT
 |
 v
Stripe PaymentIntent
 |
 v
Customer Payment
 |
 v
Stripe Webhook
 |
 v
Verify Signature
 |
 v
Webhook Idempotency
 |
 v
Payment = SUCCEEDED
 |
 v
Saga
 |
 v
Seats = BOOKED
 |
 v
Booking = CONFIRMED

Failure / expiry path:

PENDING Booking
      |
      v
Hold expires
      |
      v
BullMQ delayed job
      |
      v
Lock ShowSeat
      |
      v
Verify HELD + expired
      |
      v
Seat = AVAILABLE
      |
      v
Booking = CANCELLED
28. Final Architecture Principle

The most important design decision in SeatLock is:

Database is the final source of truth.

Redis, Redlock, cache, BullMQ and Stripe are supporting systems. The seat-booking invariant is ultimately protected by transactional PostgreSQL state changes and concurrency control.

